## TODO: pak-analyzer

Phase 1 of the pak content manifest feature. The work happens in apps/index/. Phases must
land in order: pak-analyzer -> pak-entries-schema -> manifest-backfill-and-size ->
pak-conflict-detection (the last one is the only apps/desktop change).

REWRITTEN 2026-08-01: this entry was written when the indexer was the standalone
modrex-index repo producing one index.db. The parser itself is unaffected (it reads bytes),
but every path and plumbing reference was retargeted at the Postgres/R2 pipeline.

### Context and reasoning (read before implementing)

The goal of the whole feature: know which asset paths each indexed .pak mounts, so the
app can detect conflicts between installed mods (two mods overriding the same asset),
identify repackaged mods, and verify integrity. A Merkle tree was considered and
rejected: Merkle trees pay off when a consumer needs to verify a subset without having
all the data, but the app downloads a game's whole snapshot anyway, so a flat per-pak
entry manifest gives every benefit with none of the complexity.

Engine fact that shapes everything: BOTH PAYDAY 3 and Crime Boss are Unreal Engine
4.27.2. This means the parser needs exactly one format generation: the UE 4.27 pak index
layout and the UE 4.27 IoStore layout. No UE5 variants, no version matrix.

(This entry used to carry a warning that apps/desktop/CLAUDE.md misattributed PD3 to UE5.
That has since been corrected in the docs - they now say UE 4.27 explicitly - so the
warning is dropped as of 2026-08-01.)

Why the parser lands alone, before any schema work: binary-format parsing against real
files is the only risky part of the feature. Everything else is plumbing that already
has proven patterns in postgres/process-content.ts. Isolating the risk makes it cheap to
verify and cheap to throw away if an assumption is wrong.

### What to build (in apps/index/)

The parser is a new module beside content-archive.ts, which is where archive bytes are
already turned into ContentEntry rows. It stays pure (Buffer in, parse result out) so it
is testable without Postgres or the network, the same way content-archive.ts is.

- parsePakIndex(buf: Buffer): read the FPakInfo footer at the end of the file (magic
  0x5A6F12E1), which carries the pak version and the index offset/size. Follow it to
  the pak index and return every mounted asset path. Each FPakEntry record already
  contains a SHA-1 hash of its data, so per-entry hashes come FREE - never read or hash
  the asset bytes themselves. Also return per-entry size. UE 4.25+ split the index into
  a path-hash index and a full directory index; 4.27 uses that layout, so parse the full
  directory index for paths.
- parseUtocIndex(buf: Buffer): read the .utoc directory index. For IoStore mods the
  .utoc IS the table of contents (.ucas holds the data). Crime Boss ModKit mods almost
  always ship the .pak/.ucas/.utoc triplet where the .pak is nearly empty and the real
  content list is in the .utoc; PD3 mods usually ship a bare .pak. Both parsers are
  needed.
- Explicit status instead of guessing (this is a hard rule): the parse result is one of
  ok / encrypted (pak index is encrypted - possible, mods normally are not) /
  no-directory-index (.utoc with a stripped directory index has chunk ids but no
  paths). A silent empty result would be indistinguishable from "empty pak" and would
  later make the app claim "no conflicts" for a pak whose contents are simply unknown -
  same class of bug as the zero-yield memoization gap fixed in the CI perf work.
- A dev util in the style of the existing check-pak.mjs / lookup-mod.mjs (e.g.
  analyze-pak.mjs <path>) that runs the parser on a local file and prints paths, hashes,
  sizes, status.

### Verification (do not skip)

Test against real installed mods on this machine, not spec assumptions: Crime Boss
skeletons under {gamePath}/CrimeBoss/Mods/<name>/Content/Paks/WindowsNoEditor/ (pak +
ucas/utoc triplets) and PD3 paks under {gamePath}/PAYDAY3/Content/Paks/~mods/.

To cross-check identities, read the app's own downloaded snapshots at
C:/Users/oleh/AppData/Roaming/Modrex/indexes/<game_id>.db. Note the dev utils
(check-index.mjs, lookup-mod.mjs, check-pak.mjs) still point their APP_DB constant at the
legacy single-file C:/Users/oleh/AppData/Roaming/Modrex/mod-index.db, which the current app
no longer writes. Repointing them at the per-game snapshots is a small prerequisite worth
doing first, since analyze-pak.mjs will want the same lookup.

Every parsed path list should be spot-checked against what the mod's modworkshop page
says it changes.

