## TODO: modrex-core-crate

Far-future architecture. Large. Blocked on: nexus-prototype-merge AND a concrete second
consumer existing (a CLI, automation, or the first plugin experiment). Do NOT do
preemptively - an extracted core with no second consumer is dead weight, the exact failure
mode visible in the neighboring Refract_MC repo whose packages/core is Electron-era code
the app only imports for types.

### Goal

Make the mod engine host-independent so it can back more than the Tauri app (a headless
CLI, automation, or plugins). Today the ops in commands/mods/ thread `AppHandle` through
everything, coupling the domain logic to Tauri and the renderer IPC lifecycle, and making
core logic untestable without Tauri machinery.

### Approach when the trigger fires

- The ops mostly need three narrow things from `AppHandle`: settings access, event
  emission (progress), and index lookups. Define small traits (e.g. `SettingsStore`,
  `ProgressSink`, `ModIndex`) or pass plain data, so the core takes those instead of
  `AppHandle`.
- Lift commands/mods (engine, install, state, paths, reorder, folders, zip, identify) + the
  loader specs into a `modrex-core` crate depending on none of Tauri. The Tauri app becomes
  a thin adapter implementing the traits over `AppHandle`.
- Payoff: core tests run without Tauri; a CLI or plugin host drives the same engine; the
  "its own API" idea (programmatic/headless modrex) falls out nearly for free.
- Prerequisite ordering: SATISFIED as of 2026-07-20. typed-ipc-codegen and the domain
  model both landed, so the crate's public types would be the neutral domain types in
  commands/domain.rs rather than ModWorkshop wire shapes. The remaining blocker is the
  one that always mattered: a concrete second consumer.

---

## TODO: plugin-system

Far-future ecosystem. Large. Blocked on: modrex-core-crate AND real ecosystem demand.
Security-critical: Modrex's core job is extracting attacker-controlled archives into game
directories, so plugin isolation matters unusually much.

### Goal

Let third parties extend Modrex along the axis the app already grows on: games, loaders,
and sources. Build in escalating capability, cheapest and safest first, and only build the
tier that demand actually requires.

- Tier 1 - declarative manifest plugins (do first): a game or loader definition shipped as
  DATA (a manifest conforming to the GameSpec/LoaderSpec shape), loaded at runtime. Covers
  the most likely community contribution (a new family-conforming game or a loader),
  executes NO third-party code, minimal security surface. Direct continuation of
  game-registry/loader-registry: once those are data-driven, "shippable as a manifest
  between releases" is a small step. The cross-repo duplication this used to relieve is
  already gone: packages/games is that shared manifest, and all three apps consume it.
- Tier 2 - sandboxed logic hooks (only if real ones are demanded): for behavior a manifest
  cannot express (a novel archive_resolver / enabled_sync / embedded_id_parser). Use WASM
  (wasmtime or extism), NOT webview-JS (UI access = weak isolation) and NOT native dylibs
  (ABI + security, hard no). The plugin gets a narrow capability API (the modrex-core
  traits), no ambient filesystem/network. This is where the extracted core and its trait
  boundaries pay off.
- The public plugin-api surface (cf. Refract_MC's packages/plugin-api) should be EXTRACTED
  from a working core when Tier 2 is real, never declared up front.

### The monorepo landed, so Tier 1 is now cheaper (resolved 2026-08-01)

This section used to be conditional on the ecosystem-monorepo entry. That migration was
delivered 2026-07-23, so the condition is now a fact.

`packages/games` IS the manifest this tier would load: a neutral game shape, single source
of truth, proven sufficient by three consumers (the app, the indexer, the site's docs).
What remains for Tier 1 is making that data readable from disk at runtime rather than only
as a compile-time import, plus validation. That is mostly a loader.

Caveat that survived the migration: packages/games carries game IDENTITY, not game
BEHAVIOR. A community game that needs a novel archive_resolver still cannot be expressed as
Tier 1 data until game-registry-behavior-hooks defines those hook fields, so that entry is a
soft prerequisite for Tier 1 covering anything beyond family-conforming games.

This does not change the trigger. Tier 1 still waits on real ecosystem demand, and Tier 2
still waits on modrex-core-crate, whose own trigger (a concrete second consumer of the mod
engine) the monorepo does NOT satisfy.

